<Layout
  title="Kubernetes Deployment Guide: Zero to Production"
  description="Take an application from a single Deployment manifest to a resilient, observable, autoscaling workload — covering probes, resource limits, rollouts, and common failure modes."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-content">
      <a class="back" href="/guides/">← Back to Guides</a>
      <span class="eyebrow">Kubernetes · Guide</span>
      <h1>Kubernetes Deployment Guide: Zero to Production</h1>
      <p>
        A working Deployment manifest and a production-ready one look
        similar on the surface. The difference is a handful of fields
        that determine whether your application survives a bad day.
      </p>
      <div class="meta"><span>16 min read</span><span>·</span><span>Assumes a working cluster and kubectl access</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-wrap">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <h2>On this page</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#minimal">Start with a minimal Deployment</a></li>
        <li><a href="#probes">Add health probes before anything else</a></li>
        <li><a href="#resources">Set resource requests and limits</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rollouts">Configure a safe rollout strategy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#service">Expose it with a Service</a></li>
        <li><a href="#scaling">Add autoscaling once you have real traffic data</a></li>
        <li><a href="#failures">Failure modes to know before they happen</a></li>
        <li><a href="#checklist">The production-readiness checklist</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="minimal">Start with a minimal Deployment</h2>
    <p>
      Every Kubernetes workload starts as a Deployment, which manages a
      set of identical Pods and handles replacing them when they fail or
      when you roll out a new version.
    </p>
    <pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: myapp
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: myapp
          image: registry.example.com/myapp:1.4.0</code></pre>
    <p>
      This runs, but it isn't production-ready yet. Kubernetes has no way
      to know if the application inside the container is actually healthy,
      no limit on how much CPU or memory it can consume, and no defined
      strategy for how a new version replaces the old one. Each of the
      following sections closes one of those gaps.
    </p>

    <h2 id="probes">Add health probes before anything else</h2>
    <p>
      Without probes, Kubernetes only knows whether the container process
      is running — not whether the application inside it is actually
      working. A hung process that never crashes will keep receiving
      traffic forever unless a probe tells Kubernetes otherwise.
    </p>
    <pre><code>          readinessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /healthz
              port: 8080
            initialDelaySeconds: 5
            periodSeconds: 10
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /healthz
              port: 8080
            initialDelaySeconds: 15
            periodSeconds: 20</code></pre>
    <p>
      The readiness probe controls whether the Pod receives traffic — a
      Pod that fails readiness is pulled out of the Service's endpoints
      without being killed, which is exactly what you want during a slow
      startup or a temporary downstream outage. The liveness probe
      controls whether the Pod gets restarted. Give the liveness probe a
      longer <code>initialDelaySeconds</code> than readiness, so a slow
      startup doesn't get mistaken for a hang and trigger a restart loop.
    </p>
    <p>
      For applications with a genuinely long startup time — loading a
      large cache, running migrations — add a <code>startupProbe</code>
      too, so the liveness probe doesn't start counting failures before
      the app has even finished initializing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="resources">Set resource requests and limits</h2>
    <p>
      Without requests, the scheduler has no idea how much CPU or memory a
      Pod actually needs, which leads to overcrowded nodes. Without
      limits, one misbehaving Pod can starve every other workload on the
      same node.
    </p>
    <pre><code>          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 250m
              memory: 256Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 500m
              memory: 512Mi</code></pre>
    <p>
      Set requests based on what the application actually uses under
      normal load — check with <code>kubectl top pod</code> after it's
      been running for a while, rather than guessing. A memory limit set
      too low results in the container being OOMKilled repeatedly, which
      shows up as unexplained restarts unless you specifically check the
      termination reason.
    </p>
    <pre><code>kubectl get pod myapp-xxx -o jsonpath="&lbrace;.status.containerStatuses[*].lastState.terminated.reason&rbrace;"</code></pre>

    <h2 id="rollouts">Configure a safe rollout strategy</h2>
    <p>
      The default <code>RollingUpdate</code> strategy replaces Pods
      gradually, but its default tolerances aren't always what you want
      for a small deployment. Be explicit:
    </p>
    <pre><code>  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxUnavailable: 0
      maxSurge: 1</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> means Kubernetes must bring up a new,
      healthy Pod before removing an old one — you never drop below your
      desired replica count during a deploy, at the cost of briefly
      running one extra Pod (<code>maxSurge: 1</code>). For a Deployment
      with very few replicas, this matters far more than it does at large
      scale, where losing one Pod out of fifty is unnoticeable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Watch a rollout in real time and be ready to stop it before it
      finishes if something looks wrong:
    </p>
    <pre><code>kubectl rollout status deployment/myapp
kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp</code></pre>

    <h2 id="service">Expose it with a Service</h2>
    <p>
      A Deployment alone isn't reachable — a Service provides a stable
      address and load-balances across whichever Pods are currently ready.
    </p>
    <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: myapp
spec:
  selector:
    app: myapp
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080</code></pre>
    <p>
      The <code>selector</code> here must match the Pod template's labels
      exactly — a mismatched selector is one of the most common causes of
      "the Service exists but no traffic reaches the Pods," and it fails
      silently with no error message. Confirm it's wired up correctly:
    </p>
    <pre><code>kubectl get endpoints myapp</code></pre>
    <p>
      If that command returns an empty list, the selector doesn't match
      any Ready Pods — check the labels on both sides before looking
      anywhere else.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scaling">Add autoscaling once you have real traffic data</h2>
    <p>
      Don't guess at autoscaling thresholds before you've observed real
      usage — start with a fixed replica count, gather a few days or weeks
      of <code>kubectl top pod</code> data under real traffic, then set a
      HorizontalPodAutoscaler based on what you actually saw:
    </p>
    <pre><code>apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: myapp
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: myapp
  minReplicas: 3
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70</code></pre>
    <p>
      Remember that autoscaling has a startup lag — a new Pod still needs
      to pull its image, start, and pass its readiness probe before it
      can absorb traffic. For workloads with sudden, predictable traffic
      spikes, keep <code>minReplicas</code> high enough to absorb the
      first minute of a spike before the autoscaler catches up.
    </p>

    <h2 id="failures">Failure modes to know before they happen</h2>
    <p>
      A few patterns account for most Kubernetes incidents:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> is a retry state, not the root cause — always check <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> to see why the last attempt actually died.</li>
      <li><strong>Pending Pods</strong> usually mean insufficient cluster resources, an unmatched node selector, or an unbound PersistentVolumeClaim — <code>kubectl describe pod</code> names the exact reason under Events.</li>
      <li><strong>A node going NotReady</strong> behaves very differently for stateful workloads than for this stateless Deployment — a StatefulSet Pod won't be rescheduled automatically the way this Deployment's Pods will.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="checklist">The production-readiness checklist</h2>
    <p>
      Before calling a Deployment production-ready, confirm each of these:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Readiness and liveness probes are configured and tested under a real failure.</li>
      <li>Resource requests reflect actual observed usage, and limits prevent one Pod from starving the node.</li>
      <li>The rollout strategy has been chosen deliberately, not left at the default.</li>
      <li>A PodDisruptionBudget exists if the workload can't tolerate losing more than one Pod at a time during a voluntary drain.</li>
      <li>You've run <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> at least once, successfully, before you needed it for real.</li>
    </ul>

